[Music]  what if you could walk into a pharmacy  and get meds that could actually treat  covet 19  not just the symptoms that's not an  option right now but we're one step  closer  a pharmaceutical company called merck  has been developing a pill that people  who have kova 19 could take it home  without having to go to the hospital  mark is applying for fda authorization  which could come by the end of the year  if it gets authorized by the u.s food  and drug administration it could be a  game changer  so what is this new pill and why might  it be a turning point in the fight  against covet i'm in the office doing  some digging on the topic  [Music]  up until now the main therapies for  kobit 19 like monoclonal antibodies have  all required an iv but this new  medication doesn't  patients could get a prescription from  the doctor and get that filled at their  local drugstore the same way we do for  flu or other diseases  it's called molna pirivir when someone  is diagnosed with covid or when they  have you know clear symptoms of covid  um there aren't a lot of options for  them doctors would have something to  give  patients right away to prescribe to them  that's my co-worker and fellow amherst  college alum betsy mckay  she's been covering mona piravir for  more than a year including when  promising preliminary results were first  reported in the spring and she says this  pill is a huge deal  that's because now for the first time  ever you can potentially treat covet 19  orally similar to how you take ibuprofen  but instead of taking it just once to  fend off body aches you'd take four  pills twice a day for five days  here's how it works  the goal is to stop the coronavirus from  multiplying inside your cells molna  pirovir does that by messing up the  virus's ability to make copies of itself  which prevents you from getting sick  enough that you might need to go to the  hospital it does that by basically  acting as a wrench in the virus's  replication assembly line once the virus  infects the cell it uses the cell's  machinery to copy its genetic  information over and over again that rna  contains the instructions for building  viruses that can infect and spread as  the virus copies itself molna piravir  inserts itself into these instructions  if the language isn't quite right the  message gets lost in translation new  virus particles don't form correctly and  they can't continue spreading and  infecting  good for you  bad for the virus that's why it's called  an antiviral it's similar to other  antivirals including remdezavir which is  given to covet patients through an  infusion and tamiflu which you can get  through a prescription to treat the flu  in a clinical trial the pill treatment  cut the risk of hospitalization or death  by about half when people with mild to  moderate covet took it within five days  of showing symptoms and no one died  antivirals worked best when given early  that was the problem with other covet  treatments that required an iv  by the time patients got them they were  already in the hospital  molna piravir worked so well that merck  stopped enrolling new patients in the  study after discussions with the fda to  get ready to distribute it quickly merck  has been manufacturing courses of  treatment pills already and has said it  will have 10 million ready by the end of  the year so by the end of the year  hopefully people could start getting  this drug  betsy called it a tamiflu for cobin it  would help prevent you from getting  really sick  it's also important in that it will help  reduce the amount of virus that is  spreading in the community right if  people are treated  right away  once they're infected  cuts the amount of virus in their body  and they stop spreading it it will be a  really really important tool to have  for now the drug has only been tested in  unvaccinated patients who are high risk  for covet complications but betsy said  the hope is that it works on everyone  including people who are vaccinated  because molna pyrivir disrupts a process  that's common to other rna viruses like  influenza mers and sars in theory it  could be useful to treat those too i  asked betsy if there were any concerns  she said one concern was that the side  effects of the pill are unclear merck  has released limited information about  side effects but said that drug was safe  some doctors have also expressed concern  that the way it works could also disrupt  the way our own cells work that's  because rna is important in human cells  too you have to be sure that it's  messing with the virus but not the human  cell merck said it has tested it  extensively and hasn't found that  problem  with this drug the other thing to  remember is that people would take it  for five days not an extended period  but that is one reason that um doctors  have questions about it and want to see  more data another concern  doctors are worried that having  monopyravir available could discourage  people from getting vaccinated they're  worried that people will think of this  as a replacement for vaccination but  public health experts say it's not and  neither are other similar cova drugs  that are in the pipeline an easy to take  treatment like mono piravir makes the  disease more manageable but it has to be  said that  there are people who  um took this drug in the clinical trial  and still ended up in the hospital  so  you know it's  it's not a cure-all for everybody  public health experts say that it's  better to prevent covet than treat it  that's why getting vaccinated is still  the best way to put the pandemic behind  us  if you have any questions about covid  please leave them in the comments and if  you're interested in infectious disease  or public health i've left some links to  betsy's work down below and don't forget  to hit subscribe so you get your dose of  science and health updates hasta luego  nos vemos prontos  [Music]

